theory_blocks.body:
Longer hydrocarbon chains have a greater surface area in contact with neighbouring molecules, which increases the strength of the (weak, non-bonding) intermolecular attractions between them;

more energy is then needed to separate the molecules enough to form a gas, so longer-chain hydrocarbons have higher boiling points.

This is why the column can sort hydrocarbons by chain length indirectly, simply by sorting them by boiling point: the two properties change together across the whole homologous series, not just for the few short examples named in an earlier lesson.

