theory_blocks.body:
Describing alkanes as unreactive is a comparison, not an absolute claim: compared with unsaturated hydrocarbons (alkenes, covered next) or with many other families of compounds, alkanes undergo far fewer reactions at room temperature.

Combustion is the clear exception met in this course, and it only happens because [[blue:burning supplies enough energy (an ignition source) to start breaking the strong single bonds]];

without that initial energy input, alkanes and oxygen can sit together with no reaction at all.

